  2430'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-111


                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  2431'                         EXADDR:
  2431'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2432'   32 2A8B'              	STA	SRNGF
  2435'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2436'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  2438'   CA 2461'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  243B'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-112


  243C'   CD 2476'              	CALL	EXNUM
  243F'   DA 245F'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2442'   22 2A8C'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2445'   22 2A8E'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  2448'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2449'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  244B'   C2 2458'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  244E'   13                    	INX	D
  244F'   CD 2476'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2452'   DA 245F'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  2455'   22 2A8E'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2458'                         EXA_YS:
  2458'   EB                    	XCHG
  2459'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  245B'   32 2A8B'              	STA	SRNGF
  245E'   C9                    	RET
  245F'                         EXA_NO:
  245F'   EB                    	XCHG
  2460'   C9                    	RET
  2461'                         EXA_AL:
  2461'   23                    	INX	H
  2462'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2463'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2466'   22 2A8C'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2469'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  246C'   22 2A8E'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  246F'   E1                    	POP	H
  2470'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2472'   32 2A8B'              	STA	SRNGF
  2475'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  2476'                         EXNUM:
  2476'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2477'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2479'   D8                    	RC
  247A'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  247C'   3F                    	CMC
  247D'   D8                    	RC
  247E'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2481'                         EXN_LP:
  2481'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2482'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2484'   DA 249E'              	JC	EXN_DN
  2487'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  2489'   D2 249E'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  248C'   13                    	INX	D
  248D'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  248E'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  248F'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  2491'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2492'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  2493'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  2494'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  2495'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-113


  2496'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  2497'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  2498'   D1                    	POP	D
  2499'   09                    	DAD	B
  249A'   C1                    	POP	B
  249B'   C3 2481'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  249E'                         EXN_DN:
  249E'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  249F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  24A0'                         EX_S:
  24A0'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  24A1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24A2'   CA 24E4'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  24A5'   32 2A90'              	STA	SDELIM
  24A8'   23                    	INX	H
  24A9'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  24AC'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  24AF'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  24B0'   CD 24EA'              	CALL	SCOPY
  24B3'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  24B4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24B5'   CA 24E4'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  24B8'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  24BB'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  24BD'   32 2A58'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  24C0'   11 2AA2'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  24C3'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  24C5'   CD 24EA'              	CALL	SCOPY
  24C8'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  24C9'   32 2AA1'              	STA	SREPLN
  24CC'   AF                    	XRA	A
  24CD'   32 2A91'              	STA	SGFLAG
  24D0'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  24D1'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  24D3'   C2 24DC'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  24D6'   23                    	INX	H
  24D7'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  24D9'   32 2A91'              	STA	SGFLAG
  24DC'                         EX_SG:
  24DC'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  24DD'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24DE'   C2 227B'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  24E1'   C3 250A'              	JMP	SUBGO
  24E4'                         SUBNOP:
  24E4'   21 2333'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  24E7'   C3 2289'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  24EA'                         SCOPY:
  24EA'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  24EC'                         SCP_LP:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-114


  24EC'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  24ED'   B9                    	CMP	C
  24EE'   D2 2507'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  24F1'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  24F2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24F3'   CA 2507'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  24F6'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  24F7'   21 2A90'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  24FA'   BE                    	CMP	M
  24FB'   E1                    	POP	H
  24FC'   CA 2506'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  24FF'   12                    	STAX	D
  2500'   13                    	INX	D
  2501'   23                    	INX	H
  2502'   04                    	INR	B
  2503'   C3 24EC'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  2506'                         SCP_SK:
  2506'   23                    	INX	H
  2507'                         SCP_DN:
  2507'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2508'   12                    	STAX	D
  2509'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  250A'                         SUBGO:
  250A'   CD 0F42'              	CALL	GZONE
  250D'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2510'   22 2A9F'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2513'   21 2A9C'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  2516'   CD 044F'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  2519'   AF                    	XRA	A
  251A'   32 2A92'              	STA	SANY
  251D'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2520'   22 2A94'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2523'   3A 2A8B'              	LDA	SRNGF
  2526'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2527'   CA 2539'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  252A'   CD 1E05'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  252D'   DA 25B5'              	JC	SUBBAD
  2530'   2A 2A8C'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  2533'   CD 0EB0'              	CALL	GOLN
  2536'   C3 253F'              	JMP	SUBLP
  2539'                         SUB_1L:
  2539'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  253C'   22 2A96'              	SHLD	SLINES
  253F'                         SUBLP:
  253F'   CD 25BB'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2542'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2543'   CA 2551'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  2546'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2548'   32 2A92'              	STA	SANY
  254B'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-115


  254E'   22 2A94'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2551'                         SUB_NX:
  2551'   2A 2A96'              	LHLD	SLINES
  2554'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2555'   22 2A96'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2558'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2559'   B5                    	ORA	L
  255A'   CA 2578'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  255D'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2560'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2561'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  2564'   3A 2A92'              	LDA	SANY
  2567'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2568'   CA 2572'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  256B'   2A 2A94'              	LHLD	SBACK
  256E'   23                    	INX	H
  256F'   22 2A94'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  2572'                         SUB_AE:
  2572'   CD 0A1D'              	CALL	ATEOF
  2575'   D2 253F'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  2578'                         SUBDON:
  2578'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  257B'   3A 2A92'              	LDA	SANY
  257E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  257F'   CA 259D'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  2582'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2584'   32 29D9'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  2587'   2A 2A94'              	LHLD	SBACK
  258A'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  258B'   B5                    	ORA	L
  258C'   CA 2597'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  258F'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  2591'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  2594'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  2597'                         SUB_PL:
  2597'   CD 0F5A'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  259A'   C3 077B'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  259D'                         SUBNFD:
  259D'   21 2A9C'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  25A0'   CD 03F6'              	CALL	BACKTO
  25A3'   2A 2A9F'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  25A6'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-116


  25A9'   21 2313'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  25AC'   CD 2289'              	CALL	EXERR
  25AF'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  25B2'   C3 2284'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  25B5'                         SUBBAD:
  25B5'   21 2301'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  25B8'   C3 227E'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  25BB'                         SUBLIN:
  25BB'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25BC'   32 2A93'              	STA	SLCHG
  25BF'   CD 263A'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  25C2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  25C5'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  25C5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  25C8'   DA 25D0'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  25CB'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  25CD'   C2 25C5'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  25D0'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  25D0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  25D3'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  25D4'   CD 14BE'              	CALL	CEND
  25D7'   D1                    	POP	D
  25D8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  25DB'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  25DC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  25DF'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  25E0'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  25E1'   FA 2633'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  25E4'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  25E5'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  25E6'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  25E9'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  25EC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  25EF'   DA 2633'              	JC	SUBL_D
  25F2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  25F5'   EB                    	XCHG
  25F6'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  25F9'   EB                    	XCHG
  25FA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  25FD'   EB                    	XCHG
  25FE'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  25FF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2602'   D1                    	POP	D
  2603'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  2604'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-117


                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  2607'   3A 2A92'              	LDA	SANY
  260A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  260B'   C2 2618'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  260E'   3A 2A93'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2611'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2612'   C2 2618'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2615'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  2618'                         SUBL_C:
  2618'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  261B'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  261C'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  261E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2621'   11 2AA1'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  2624'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  2627'                         SUBL_I:
  2627'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2629'   32 2A93'              	STA	SLCHG
  262C'   3A 2A91'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  262F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2630'   C2 25D0'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  2633'                         SUBL_D:
  2633'   CD 263A'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2636'   3A 2A93'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2639'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  263A'                         SUBBOL:
  263A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  263D'   CD 05E4'              	CALL	BOL
  2640'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  2643'                         VC_SRCH:
  2643'   CD 075A'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2646'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2647'   CD 068B'              	CALL	GETCNT
  264A'   22 2A81'              	SHLD	SCNT
  264D'   C1                    	POP	B
  264E'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  264F'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2651'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2653'   CA 2658'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  2656'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2658'                         VS_DIR:
  2658'   32 2A56'              	STA	SDIR
  265B'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  265E'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2661'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2662'   CD 1BA1'              	CALL	RDLINE
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-118


  2665'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2666'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  2667'   3A 2A2E'              	LDA	RLLEN
  266A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  266B'   CA 2680'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  266E'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  266F'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2671'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2674'   19                    	DAD	D
  2675'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  2677'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  2678'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  267B'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  267D'   32 2A58'              	STA	SHAVE
  2680'                         VS_OLD:
  2680'   3A 2A58'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2683'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2684'   CA 2690'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  2687'   3A 2A56'              	LDA	SDIR
  268A'   32 2A57'              	STA	SDIRE
  268D'   C3 26B9'              	JMP	SRCHN
  2690'                         VS_NOP:
  2690'   21 2333'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2693'   C3 2289'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  2696'                         VC_N:
  2696'   CD 075A'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2699'   3A 2A56'              	LDA	SDIR
  269C'   C3 26A6'              	JMP	VN_GO
  269F'                         VCB_N:
  269F'   CD 075A'              	CALL	SETMOV
  26A2'   3A 2A56'              	LDA	SDIR
  26A5'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  26A6'                         VN_GO:
  26A6'   32 2A57'              	STA	SDIRE
  26A9'   CD 068B'              	CALL	GETCNT
  26AC'   22 2A81'              	SHLD	SCNT
  26AF'   3A 2A58'              	LDA	SHAVE
  26B2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26B3'   CA 2690'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  26B6'   C3 26B9'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  26B9'                         SRCHN:
  26B9'   CD 0F42'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  26BC'   CD 06B4'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and a ^C may stop it
  26BF'                         SN_LP:
  26BF'   CD 26F4'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  26C2'   DA 26DF'              	JC	SN_BAD
  26C5'   2A 2A81'              	LHLD	SCNT
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-119


  26C8'   2B                    	DCX	H
  26C9'   22 2A81'              	SHLD	SCNT
  26CC'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  26CD'   B5                    	ORA	L
  26CE'   C2 26BF'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  26D1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  26D4'   CD 06EB'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  26D7'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  26D9'   32 2A63'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  26DC'   C3 0F5A'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  26DF'                         SN_BAD:
  26DF'   CD 06EB'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  26E2'   DA 0703'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  26E5'   CD 06F6'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  26E8'   21 2313'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  26EB'   CD 2289'              	CALL	EXERR
  26EE'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  26F1'   C3 2284'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  26F4'                         SRCHGO:
  26F4'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  26F6'   32 2A8A'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  26F9'   CD 270F'              	CALL	SRCHW
  26FC'   D0                    	RNC
  26FD'   CD 06EF'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2700'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2701'   3A 2A57'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2704'   2F                    	CMA
  2705'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2708'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2709'   32 2A8A'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  270C'   C3 270F'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  270F'                         SRCHW:
  270F'   3A 2A57'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2712'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2715'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2718'   22 29D6'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  271B'   AF                    	XRA	A
  271C'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  271F'                         SW_LP:
  271F'   CD 06EF'              	CALL	BRKQ		; a ^C spent the count, here or on the way to
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-120


  2722'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2723'   CD 272F'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2726'   D0                    	RNC
  2727'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  272A'   D2 271F'              	JNC	SW_LP
  272D'   37                    	STC
  272E'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  272F'                         SRCH1:
  272F'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2732'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2735'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2736'   FA 275A'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2739'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  273C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  273F'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2742'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2743'   3A 2A8A'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2746'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2747'   CA 2750'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  274A'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  274B'   B1                    	ORA	C
  274C'   37                    	STC
  274D'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  274E'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  274F'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2750'                         SR1_FW:
  2750'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2753'   D8                    	RC
  2754'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2757'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  275A'                         SR1_BK:
  275A'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  275D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2760'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2763'   D8                    	RC
  2764'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2767'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  276A'   D8                    	RC
  276B'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-121


                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  276E'                         MSGNAM:
  276E'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  276F'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2771'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2774'   E1                    	POP	H
  2775'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2776'   23                    	INX	H
  2777'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2778'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  277A'   C2 2787'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  277D'   21 23A1'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2780'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2783'   E1                    	POP	H
  2784'   C3 27D1'              	JMP	MN_END
  2787'                         MN_HAS:
  2787'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2789'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  278C'   E1                    	POP	H
  278D'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  278E'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  278F'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2790'   CA 279E'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2793'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2794'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2796'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2799'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  279B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  279E'                         MN_NAM:
  279E'   E1                    	POP	H
  279F'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27A0'   23                    	INX	H
  27A1'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  27A3'                         MN_NM:
  27A3'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27A4'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27A6'   CA 27B1'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  27A9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27AC'   23                    	INX	H
  27AD'   0D                    	DCR	C
  27AE'   C2 27A3'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  27B1'                         MN_EXT:
  27B1'   E1                    	POP	H
  27B2'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  27B5'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-122


  27B6'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27B7'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27B9'   CA 27D1'              	JZ	MN_END
  27BC'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  27BE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27C1'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  27C3'                         MN_XL:
  27C3'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27C4'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27C6'   CA 27D1'              	JZ	MN_END
  27C9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27CC'   23                    	INX	H
  27CD'   0D                    	DCR	C
  27CE'   C2 27C3'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  27D1'                         MN_END:
  27D1'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  27D3'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  27D6'                         MSGFLG:
  27D6'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  27D8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  27D9'   CA 27E9'              	JZ	MF_RO
  27DC'   3A 29D9'              	LDA	MODF
  27DF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  27E0'   CA 27E9'              	JZ	MF_RO
  27E3'   21 23AB'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  27E6'   CD 27FE'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  27E9'                         MF_RO:
  27E9'   3A 2A44'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  27EC'   B7                    	ORA	A
  27ED'   CA 27F6'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  27F0'   21 23B6'              	LXI	H,SROM
  27F3'   CD 27FE'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  27F6'                         MF_NEW:
  27F6'   3A 2A42'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  27F9'   B7                    	ORA	A
  27FA'   C8                    	RZ
  27FB'   21 23C1'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  27FE'                         MF_PUT:
  27FE'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  27FF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2800'   C2 280C'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2803'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2805'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2806'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2808'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  280B'   E1                    	POP	H
  280C'                         MF_STR:
  280C'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-123


                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  280F'                         EMITW:
  280F'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2810'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2811'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2813'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2816'   CD 2834'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2819'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  281C'   CD 2834'              	CALL	EWDIG
  281F'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2822'   CD 2834'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2825'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2828'   CD 2834'              	CALL	EWDIG
  282B'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  282C'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  282E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2831'   D1                    	POP	D
  2832'   C1                    	POP	B
  2833'   C9                    	RET
  2834'                         EWDIG:
  2834'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2836'                         EW_LP:
  2836'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2837'   93                    	SUB	E
  2838'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2839'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  283A'   9A                    	SBB	D
  283B'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  283C'   DA 2843'              	JC	EW_DN
  283F'   0C                    	INR	C
  2840'   C3 2836'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2843'                         EW_DN:
  2843'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2844'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2845'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2846'   C2 284C'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2849'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  284A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  284B'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  284C'                         EW_PR:
  284C'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  284D'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  284F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2852'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2854'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2855'                         ISEMPT:
  2855'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2858'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2859'   B5                    	ORA	L
  285A'   C0                    	RNZ
  285B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  285E'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  285F'   B5                    	ORA	L
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-124


  2860'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2861'                         MSGCTG:
  2861'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2864'   CD 276E'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2867'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2869'   CD 27D6'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  286C'   CD 2855'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  286F'   C2 2878'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2872'   21 23D4'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2875'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2878'                         MG_POS:
  2878'   21 23C7'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  287B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  287E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2881'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2882'   CD 280F'              	CALL	EMITW
  2885'   21 23CE'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2888'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  288B'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  288E'   23                    	INX	H
  288F'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2890'   CD 280F'              	CALL	EMITW
  2893'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2896'   23                    	INX	H
  2897'   D1                    	POP	D
  2898'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  289B'   C8                    	RZ
  289C'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  289D'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  289F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  28A2'   E1                    	POP	H
  28A3'   C3 280F'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  28A6'                         FILMSG:
  28A6'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  28A9'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  28AB'   C8                    	RZ
  28AC'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  28AF'   CD 276E'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  28B2'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  28B3'   C3 27D6'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  28B6'                         WRMSG:
  28B6'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  28B9'   CD 276E'              	CALL	MSGNAM
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


  28BC'   21 23E6'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  28BF'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  28C2'                         MSGPST:
  28C2'   3A 2A41'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  28C5'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28C6'   C8                    	RZ
  28C7'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  28C8'   AF                    	XRA	A
  28C9'   32 2A41'              	STA	MSGWHT
  28CC'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  28CD'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  28CF'   CA 28EB'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  28D2'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  28D4'   CA 28F1'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  28D7'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  28D9'   CA 28E2'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  28DC'   CD 2861'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  28DF'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  28E2'                         MP_BIG:
  28E2'   21 2382'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  28E5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  28E8'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  28EB'                         MP_FIL:
  28EB'   CD 28A6'              	CALL	FILMSG
  28EE'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  28F1'                         MP_WRT:
  28F1'   CD 28B6'              	CALL	WRMSG
  28F4'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  28F7'                         VC_CTLG:
  28F7'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
